Write-ThemeFile 'template-parts/content-blog-preview.php' @'
<div class=\"container journal-layout section\">
    <aside class=\"journal-index\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">IN THIS NOTEBOOK</p><a href=\"#packing\">01 / Pack for the pauses</a><a href=\"#weather\">02 / Leave room for weather</a><a href=\"#etiquette\">03 / Share the path</a><?php rr_photo(); ?></aside>
    <div class=\"journal-entries\">
        <article id=\"packing\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">01 / PACKING / 3 MIN READ</p><h2>Pack for the pauses.</h2><p class=\"lead\">It is easy to pack for moving. Remember the moments when you stop.</p><p>A warm layer earns its place when you sit down for lunch. Waterproofs belong near the top of your bag, where you can reach them before a shower becomes a soaking. Keep spare clothing dry inside a protective bag.</p><p>Take familiar food, enough water for the planned day and a little extra. Bring personal medication, sun protection and a charged phone. Wear boots you already know, rather than testing a new pair on a long outing.</p><p>Pack the night before, then check the plan and forecast in the morning. This is a starting list; the route, season and individual needs determine the final kit.</p></article>
        <article id=\"weather\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">02 / PLANNING / 2 MIN READ</p><h2>Leave room for weather.</h2><p class=\"lead\">A forecast is part of the plan, not a promise from the sky.</p><p>Look at the whole day, including the return journey. Conditions on a ridge can differ from those in the valley. Consider wind, visibility and the temperature during a long stop, as well as whether rain is expected.</p><p>Choose an alternative before leaving: a sheltered walk, a shorter day or a different date. Decide when to reassess, and make it easy for everyone to say they are uncomfortable. Getting home with a good story does not require reaching the highest point.</p></article>
        <article id=\"etiquette\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">03 / ON THE TRAIL / 2 MIN READ</p><h2>Share the path.</h2><p class=\"lead\">The outdoors is more welcoming when we leave space for each other.</p><p>Pause where others can pass without stepping into fragile vegetation. Keep voices considerate, give animals room and follow posted guidance about dogs and access. Leave gates as instructed at the location.</p><p>Carry litter and food scraps home. A quiet greeting, a patient wait at a narrow section and a little distance from wildlife are small choices that make a difference. Follow the local countryside guidance wherever you walk.</p></article>
    </div>
</div>
'@
