# Ridge & River changelog

## 1.0.0

- Introduced a six-section landscape-led home page with numbered trail rows, difficulty guidance, guiding ethos, packing notes and an inquiry link.
- Added complete story, walks, field notes, journal, contact, privacy and ridge-walk layouts.
- Replaced navigation with direct desktop links and an accessible mobile disclosure.
- Added local-only inquiry feedback, subtle motion and reduced-motion support.
- Applied pine, parchment and rust styling to templates, editor palette and interface marks.
- Removed unrelated starter illustrations and inactive submission handlers.
'@
Write-ThemeFile 'docs/getting-started.md' @'
# Getting started with Ridge & River

Activate the prepared theme in WordPress. Create the ordinary pages listed in README.md with their exact slugs. Make featured a child of services. The slug templates provide sample content without rewriting requests or creating database records. A static front page is optional because front-page.php already supplies the home layout.

Run npm run build after SCSS or JavaScript edits. This command writes only the two local runtime bundles.

Browse each requested page and test navigation with keyboard and touch. The fictional route descriptions are not navigable itineraries. No dates, prices, credentials or real meeting points are asserted.
'@
Write-ThemeFile 'docs/customization.md' @'
# Customizing Ridge & River

Palette tokens live in src/scss/abstracts/_variables.scss and theme.json. Layouts use system sans-serif type and compact monospace labels. The main stylesheet imports only the modules used by this design.

Edit sample walk data in inc/helpers.php; detailed page copy lives in page-templates and template-parts. The header and footer use the canonical WordPress page paths. Preserve the ordinary same-frame links.

The stock mountain photograph is the only approved photography. Keep its footer credit and provenance manifest. Local SVG marks are small interface decorations, not landscape substitutes.

The inquiry form intentionally has no delivery or storage handler. A real inquiry service would need a separately configured plugin, accurate privacy information and clear consent behavior. Do not change the demonstration message to imply delivery.

Run npm run build after changing source styles or interactions.
'@
Write-ThemeFile 'accessibility/README.md' @'
# Ridge & River accessibility

The header includes a visible-on-focus skip link and native navigation links. On mobile, a real button controls an in-flow disclosure with accurate aria-expanded and aria-controls. Escape closes the menu and returns focus to the toggle; focus leaving the header closes it. The disclosure is non-modal and does not trap focus. A noscript navigation remains available.

FAQs use native details and summary. Inquiry fields have visible labels and native validity checks. Demo feedback is announced through a status region and never claims delivery. No user-entered data is transmitted or persisted.

Motion uses short translations only; content is never initially hidden. Reduced-motion preferences remove transitions and animations. Meaningful landscape alternatives describe the photo; decorative marks have empty alternatives.

Responsive rules explicitly constrain hero type, controls, images and footer columns. Manual browser and assistive-technology verification should accompany deployment; these notes are design intent, not certification.
'@
Write-ThemeFile 'assets/icons/README.md' @'
# Ridge & River interface marks

mark-1.svg is the ridge-and-river brand mark. mark-2.svg is a compass-inspired wayfinding detail. mark-3.svg is a simple trail marker. These local decorative SVGs use the theme palette, have no external references and are never used as primary photography.

The original seeded asset provenance remains in assets/images/asset-manifest.json. The SVG presentations were personalized for this theme.
'@
